SYNTH-0001♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Ramirez♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Data & Society Research Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔Data & Society Research Institute⏎1 Whitehall Street, 15th Floor⏎New York, NY 10004⏎contact@datasociety.net⏎⏎January 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information, Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI⏎⏎Data & Society urges HHS to condition any reimbursement incentives for clinical AI on mandatory pre-deployment bias auditing, because documented harms to vulnerable populations already outpace existing governance capacity.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Lee♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔American Association for Respiratory Care♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Association for Respiratory Care, HHS must condition any AI incentive policy on independent workforce impact assessments before accelerating adoption that primarily serves hospital cost-extraction over patient care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0003♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Jackson♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda Jackson⏎14 Birchwood Lane⏎Middletown, CT 06457⏎⏎March 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎⏎My mother spent three months being passed between specialists because an automated screening flag sent everyone running, and not one of them pulled her old films until a semi-retired radiologist in Middletown finally did it by hand and told us nothing had changed. The people who profit from these systems are not the people who get hurt when they fail. I'm asking HHS to put real accountability structures in place before pushing adoption, not after.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Amanda Jackson♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0004♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Ramirez♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔Rutgers University♔♔♔♔♔♔EVIDENCE STANDARDS⏎⏎Rutgers University's health economics working group urges HHS to require real-world validation before approving AI-driven prior authorization tools. Documented denial rates in automated systems exceed human reviewer benchmarks, producing measurable harm to elderly and low-income patients.⏎⏎OVERSIGHT REQUIREMENTS⏎⏎Inaction on mandatory human review requirements is a regulatory choice with identifiable victims.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0005♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Rodriguez♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔Commonwealth Fund♔♔♔♔♔♔The Commonwealth Fund's affiliated dental advocacy network documented a 2.3 times higher AI-driven denial rate in federally designated shortage areas, demonstrating that accelerating adoption without independent governance mechanisms first produces measurable, compounding harm to the most vulnerable patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0006♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Young♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My doctor's office in Cascade County started using one of these AI screening systems eight months back, and I didn't even know until I asked why my mammogram results felt like a form letter. Two of the twelve women flagged the same week got wrong calls. I read about that big insurance company using a computer to automatically deny nursing home care to elderly patients. People here in rural Montana get a denial letter and assume the insurance company must be right. Require human review before any AI denial stands, and give patients a real way to appeal.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0007♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Harris♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔National Association of Emergency Medical Technicians♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Association of Emergency Medical Technicians, we submit these comments with serious concern that accelerating AI adoption without confronting who bears the cost of that acceleration will harm the frontline workers and patients our members serve daily.⏎⏎HHS should require independent audits before any deployment incentives are extended. The workers most exposed to AI-driven surveillance and job displacement are disproportionately women and workers of color, and that pattern will not self-correct.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0008♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Lee♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy is 82 years old, lives alone in Muncie, and had a stroke fourteen months ago. I am her home health aide. I am the person who notices when her left hand trembles differently than it did the week before, or when she's not finishing her sentences the way she used to. Last November, a computer system at her insurance company flagged her as low priority for a post-stroke follow-up. I spent two weeks calling her doctor's office before a human being actually looked at her chart. Two weeks. She could have had another stroke in that time.⏎⏎HHS is asking how to speed up AI adoption in clinical care, and I genuinely don't understand why that is the question. The question should be: what happens to people like Dorothy when the algorithm is wrong and nobody checks it? Insurance companies are already running automated denial systems at scale. It has been in the news. ProPublica and others have reported on it. The patients who don't appeal aren't satisfied, they're exhausted, or scared, or they don't speak English, or they don't have someone like me making calls for them. HHS has the authority to require real human review before these automated decisions stick. Use it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0009♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Hall♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔Rutgers Robert Wood Johnson Medical School♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert Hall, Biostatistician, Rutgers Robert Wood Johnson Medical School, submits these comments urging HHS to condition any AI adoption acceleration on mandatory, prospective bias auditing requirements before deployment across clinically diverse populations.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0010♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Miller♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Providence Health & Services♔♔♔♔♔♔Providence Health & Services⏎1801 Lind Avenue SW⏎Renton, WA 98057⏎⏎April 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Providence Health & Services submits these comments drawing on our experience operating a multi-state clinic network and, more specifically, on a 2024 internal compliance review that left our regulatory affairs team with a familiar and uncomfortable conclusion: the sector's appetite for deploying AI tools is running well ahead of its capacity to validate them in real clinical environments. We have seen this pattern before. EHR rollouts, sepsis prediction algorithms, duplicate alert cascades that clinicians learned to ignore entirely. Acceleration without adequate real-world performance standards is not a neutral policy choice.⏎⏎What concerns our organization most, responding to the RFI's questions on governance and reimbursement frameworks, is the absence of any structured expectation for post-deployment performance reporting. Our Q3 2024 vendor survey of 22 technology partners identified the following recurring gaps:⏎⏎o No standardized mechanism for tracking tool performance drift after go-live⏎o Payer documentation requirements that vary enough across three major relationships to require entirely separate evaluation submissions for the same product⏎o Compliance staff averaging 14 hours per new tool deployment on documentation alone⏎⏎HHS should, at minimum, require that reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical tools be conditioned on demonstrated real-world validation data, not just pre-deployment bench testing.⏎⏎Providence Health & Services Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 2⏎⏎Providence Health & Services | jessica.miller@providence.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0011♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Wright♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔Center for Responsible AI in Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The Center for Responsible AI in Medicine urges HHS to halt expansion of AI-driven prior authorization systems until mandatory human review requirements are in place. Automated denial tools like NaviHealth's algorithm have already harmed elderly and low-income patients who cannot navigate appeals.⏎⏎HHS has existing authority to act. Choosing acceleration over accountability is a decision, not a default.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0012♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Hernandez♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔National Association for Home Care and Hospice♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Association for Home Care and Hospice, we urge HHS to require independent deployment-environment evaluation before any AI clinical tool reaches our members' patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0013♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Garcia♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔Abt Associates♔♔♔♔♔♔Abt Associates appreciates the opportunity to respond to this RFI but urges HHS to reframe acceleration as precautionary governance before democratic deliberation about AI's proper role in clinical care has meaningfully occurred.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0014♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Wright♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My husband Earl was finally diagnosed with early-stage lymphoma in Portland after two misses elsewhere, and those eight months we lost still sit with me. What caught it was a specialist who noticed he was downplaying his exhaustion, something no algorithm would have picked up. Please keep real physician review mandatory at every decision point. Speed without careful human eyes behind it is not better care, it is just faster mistakes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0015♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Johnson♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister Keisha spent eight months getting told her symptoms were stress, anxiety, something she was imagining, before a specialist in Jackson finally figured out she had lupus. Part of what went wrong was a system moving too fast to pay careful attention to a Black woman in a rural Mississippi clinic. I think about that a lot when I read about AI being pushed into hospitals and doctor's offices, because I genuinely want it to work. I do. But what I keep coming back to is a question nobody seems to want to answer directly: when the AI gets it wrong, who is actually responsible?⏎⏎In my classroom, I can't have a situation where every adult in the building points at someone else when a child gets hurt. That's not a system, that's just a way of making sure nobody is ever accountable. From what I can tell, that's exactly where we're headed with AI in medicine. The doctor says the software recommended it, the hospital says the vendor is responsible, and the vendor's contract says they're not liable for clinical decisions. The patient, or the patient's family, is left with nothing. HHS should not be accelerating adoption of these tools until there is a clear, plain answer to that question, and I mean an answer that an actual patient can use, not one buried in a licensing agreement somewhere.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0016♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Green♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Arcadia♔♔♔♔♔♔April 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information -- Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎⏎Arcadia submits these comments with a straightforward question that we have not seen clearly answered in the RFI documentation: is this a proposed rule or a final rule? The distinction matters considerably to our organization and to the health systems we support, and the public-facing materials have not made it plain.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Amanda Green⏎Healthcare Administrator, Arcadia⏎Raleigh, North Carolina♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0017♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Nguyen♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔University of Nebraska Medical Center♔♔♔♔♔♔University of Nebraska Medical Center | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎ACCOUNTABILITY BEFORE ACCELERATION⏎⏎HHS cannot responsibly accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings while liability for algorithmically-driven patient harm remains practically unassignable to any solvent defendant.⏎⏎University of Nebraska Medical Center | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Mitchell♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔Atomwise♔♔♔♔♔♔Atomwise submits these comments with particular concern about payer-side AI tools currently generating coverage denials at scale. Our operational experience across 14 consortium institutions documented roughly 340 administrative hours annually per site spent reconciling AI-generated denials that contradict clinical judgment. Before HHS endorses further acceleration of AI in insurance functions, the Department should require documented human review of any AI-generated coverage determination, with explainability standards accessible to patients and enforceable appeal rights for populations least positioned to self-advocate.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0019♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Harris♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: RE: HHS AI comment submission -- Andrew H.⏎⏎My buddy Dex, out on the Kenai Peninsula, got flagged by some clinic software as low priority for a follow-up scan after a workplace injury. Three months passed before a doctor actually looked at his case. What started as a possible soft tissue problem had gotten a lot worse by then. Nobody could tell him how the computer made that call or who he was supposed to complain to. That's what I keep thinking about when I see HHS talking about moving faster on AI adoption. If the boss can save money by cutting a position, he's going to cut it, and he'll use whatever tool lets him justify that. The people doing scheduling, prior authorization, coding work -- a lot of them are women and people of color, and they're not the ones in the room deciding whether to deploy these systems. I've heard the argument that AI reduces burnout, and maybe it does somewhere, but I'm skeptical any of that actually reaches the people on the ground. Out here we don't have backup options. Please require human review and make sure someone specific is accountable when the system gets it wrong.⏎⏎-- Andrew Harris⏎Soldotna, AK 99669♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0020♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Martinez♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: Public Comment Submission -- HHS AI RFI (forwarding to docket per Sara's instructions)⏎⏎My mother was misdiagnosed for eight months in a small Iowa clinic. When I read about UnitedHealth using NaviHealth algorithms to deny rehab stays, I thought: who actually looked at her file? Someone should have to. That's all I'm asking.⏎⏎--⏎Steven Martinez♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0021♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Williams♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔National Rural Health Association♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Rural Health Association, we want to be direct: the structural problem driving inadequate rural care is pharmacy benefit manager consolidation, and this RFI does not touch it. Our 1,200 member organizations have watched PBM intermediaries extract margins from rural clinical settings for years while workforce shortages go unaddressed.⏎⏎AI adoption cannot substitute for that missing policy action.⏎⏎NRHA urges HHS to clarify how AI investment interacts with existing reimbursement distortions before directing rural providers toward technology that may primarily benefit the same consolidated intermediaries already straining their operations.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Jackson♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔Van Andel Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔Van Andel Institute respectfully urges HHS to require site-specific demographic performance validation before any AI diagnostic tool qualifies for reimbursement eligibility, given documented disparities in algorithm accuracy across underrepresented patient populations.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0023♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Sanchez♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My client Maria, a 47-year-old farmworker in Yakima, spent three months bouncing between automated screening tools that kept flagging her as low risk before a human physician finally caught her early-stage cervical cancer. I sat with her through that whole process. She moves seasonally, has no primary care doctor, and her records were split across two different counties. The AI kept asking questions that didn't fit her situation at all. And what I keep coming back to, professionally, is that this wasn't a glitch. This is what happens when systems are built around patients who have stable housing and consistent records. Now I hear about the NaviHealth situation, where insurers were using AI to deny post-acute care, and I recognize it immediately from my own caseload. Before HHS accelerates any of this, I need to see:⏎⏎1. Mandatory human review before any AI-generated denial becomes final⏎2. Appeal rights that are actually accessible, not buried in paperwork⏎3. Performance data broken out for seasonal and migrant workers specifically⏎⏎Failing to act on those safeguards isn't neutral.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0024♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Thompson♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔Meditech♔♔♔♔♔♔Meditech submits these comments with specific concern about the framing of this RFI, which treats acceleration as an unambiguous good without asking who bears the cost when deployment goes wrong. Based on our organization's ongoing work with health technology clients across New Hampshire and the broader region, the financial incentives driving AI adoption inside health systems are heavily weighted toward headcount reduction and margin improvement, not care quality. In Q3 2024, our regulatory affairs working group found that deployment decisions are routinely made at the CFO or operations level, with little documented input from frontline staff. The roles absorbing the most displacement pressure are administrative coordinators, prior authorization staff, and medical coders, positions held disproportionately by women and workers of color. HHS should require that any AI deployment in clinical or administrative settings include documented evidence of worker input and independent outcome evaluation before⏎⏎Meditech Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎reimbursement or certification pathways are extended.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0025♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Wilson♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My son Marcus was nearly lost to two wrong diagnoses even with attentive physicians reviewing every step, so please slow this down and build real human oversight requirements before any clinical AI deployment reaches patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0026♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Thompson♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔American Public Health Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Public Health Association⏎800 I Street, NW⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information -- Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎The American Public Health Association submits this comment with genuine concern about the framing embedded in this RFI. A 2024 survey across our member network found that 63% of facilities reported patient complaints tied to AI-driven clinical tools, with complaints concentrated among Medicaid-enrolled and limited-English-proficiency populations. Fewer than 30% had formal informed-consent protocols addressing algorithmic decision-making.⏎⏎APHA does not oppose AI in clinical settings. What concerns our members is the accelerationist posture this RFI assumes before those foundational equity questions are resolved. Community health centers operating with limited compliance staff told us repeatedly that bias-auditing resources are simply not available at the pace deployment is happening. HHS should reorient this framework around precautionary, human-centered principles before any formal acceleration timeline is set.⏎⏎apha_rfi_comments_oct2024@apha.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0027♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Perez♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty years as a nurse and the thing that keeps me up at night anymore is this — I had a patient, Gerald, 67 years old, cardiac history, and a decision tool flagged him low risk and three days later he was back in the ER half dead. Nobody on my floor could explain why the algortihm weighted his indicators that way. Nobody. And now HHS wants to accelerate more of this? Fine, but I keep getting junk mail about Medicare Advantage plans I never requested and cannot opt out of no matter how many times I call, and that feels connected somehow, like the whole system is designed so patients just give up.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0028♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Thompson♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My neighbor Evelyn is eighty-three years old and, as her daughter likes to say, sharp as a tack. She spent the better part of four months being passed between three specialists before a young resident, one who actually sat down with her paper records and listened to her describe her symptoms in her own halting words, caught the autoimmune condition that her initial bloodwork had flagged and then buried somewhere in an algorithm's confidence interval. I think about that a lot. I think about what it would have felt like to be Evelyn, to be frightened and tired and reduced, in the eyes of a system, to a probability score that fell just below the threshold for follow-up.⏎⏎I am a retired librarian from Tennessee, and I want to be careful not to overstate my expertise here. I am not a clinician. But I have spent forty years thinking about the difference between information retrieved and wisdom applied, and I believe that distinction matters enormously in medicine.⏎⏎The question this Request for Information is really asking, beneath all the language about adoption and acceleration, is what kind of care patients deserve. I am not sure that question is being asked directly enough.⏎⏎Research on what clinicians call the therapeutic alliance, including work by Moerman and Jonas on the meaning response in clinical encounters, suggests that physician presence, empathy, and the felt sense of being known by another person independently predict patient recovery and adherence to treatment. This is not a soft finding. The relationship itself carries measurable clinical value. When we replace that relationship, or even thin it out, with machine-generated recommendations delivered through a screen, we are not simply changing the delivery mechanism. We are changing what the patient receives.⏎⏎What I want HHS and ONC to understand is this: patients arrive at appointments scared, often inarticulate, carrying worries they cannot quite name. A clinician who is present, who reads the hesitation before an answer, the way someone holds their coat in their lap, the daughter sitting in the corner who shakes her head slightly when her mother says she's been sleeping fine, that clinician is doing diagnostic work that has no data field. There is no checkbox for what a good doctor senses in a room.⏎⏎If we design systems that route clinical judgment through algorithmic recommendations, and if those recommendations carry enough institutional weight that clinicians feel pressure to defer to them, we will have done something I find genuinely troubling. We will have transformed the patient from a person into a data point. The efficiency will be real. The loss will also be real, and it will fall hardest on patients like Evelyn, whose conditions don't present cleanly, whose stories take time to tell.⏎⏎I am not opposed to AI in medicine. I want to be clear about that. There are obvious applications, scheduling, flagging missed screenings, reducing documentation burden, where the technology could free clinicians to spend more time doing the thing that only they can do. I would support that.⏎⏎What I am asking, specifically, is that any reimbursement or certification framework HHS develops include the following:⏎⏎1. Explicit requirements that AI tools in clinical settings function as decision support, not decision replacement.⏎2. Protections ensuring that the time a clinician spends with a patient is not reduced as a consequence of AI efficiency gains.⏎3. Some form of patient disclosure when AI has materially shaped a diagnostic or treatment recommendation.⏎⏎Evelyn's resident had about twenty minutes with her, which almost wasn't enough.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0029♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Flores♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔American Academy of Allergy, Asthma and Immunology♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Academy of Allergy, Asthma and Immunology (AAAAI), we want to raise a concern that has come up repeatedly among our members serving patients in rural states, including Montana: accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings without clear reimbursement guidance will almost certainly shift costs toward patients who are least prepared to absorb them. Our members have documented cases where algorithmic tools were deployed without any corresponding update to billing structures, leaving practice administrators without a clear accountability path when something goes wrong.⏎⏎AAAAI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎HHS should not move forward on accelerating adoption until CMS has issued explicit guidance on how AI-assisted clinical decisions interact with existing fee schedules. Fixed-income patients on Medicare or Medicaid have no mechanism to anticipate whether an AI-assisted visit changes what they owe.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0030♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔King♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister spent fourteen months being told her fatigue and joint pain were stress, maybe anxiety, possibly just something she needed to manage better, and by the time a rheumatologist at Brigham and Women's finally put the pieces together, her kidneys had taken damage that will follow her the rest of her life. I think about that constantly when I read materials like this RFI, and what I keep returning to is not whether AI could have caught what her earlier doctors missed, but whether a system optimized for speed and throughput would have given any individual clinician more time to sit with her, to notice that she looked frightened, to ask the question that didn't fit the intake form. I genuinely don't know the answer to that and I'm suspicious of anyone who says they do.⏎⏎The Department's framing in this RFI treats adoption speed as the primary metric of success and I think that is the wrong question to be organizing around. Speed matters in some contexts, yes. But my sister's misdiagnosis was not a processing problem. It was a listening problem, a pattern-recognition-under-uncertainty problem, the kind that no algorithm trained on clean labeled data is well positioned to solve because the signal was ambiguous and she herself couldn't fully articulate what was wrong. She was scared and she minimized. A good clinician reads that. I am not confident a risk-scoring tool does.⏎⏎There is research, I believe from Kelley and colleagues among others, showing that physician empathy and relational continuity independently predict patient adherence and recovery, separate from technical diagnostic accuracy. That finding matters here because it means the therapeutic relationship is not just a nice thing, it is doing actual clinical work. When we replace human presence with machine-generated recommendations, even technically accurate ones, we are not simply substituting one delivery mechanism for another. We are removing something that had measurable value, and I worry the RFI does not reckon with that trade-off at all.⏎⏎What concerns me specifically is the illusion of objectivity that AI systems create. A probability score or a flagged finding looks authoritative in a way that a clinician's uncertain judgment does not. But my sister's case was full of findings that, in isolation, didn't flag anything. The system looked clean. The system was wrong. I worry that deploying AI without rigorous human override culture will make doctors less likely to trust their own discomfort with a case, because the algorithm said she was probably fine.⏎⏎I am also thinking about women specifically and about how well-documented it is that medical datasets have historically underweighted women's symptom presentations, particularly for autoimmune conditions. If we accelerate AI adoption without first auditing whether these tools perform equitably across patient populations, we are going to bake existing diagnostic disparities into the infrastructure of care and make them harder to see because they'll look like objective outputs rather than human error.⏎⏎My specific ask is this: before HHS prioritizes throughput metrics or adoption rates, the Department should fund research into how AI deployment affects the patient experience of care, including whether patients feel seen and heard, whether clinicians feel they have time to exercise independent judgment, and whether relational continuity is being preserved or quietly eroded. Diagnostic accuracy matters but it is not the whole of what medicine is supposed to do, and I think the RFI would benefit from a question that takes that seriously, maybe something as simple as asking what we lose when we♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Gonzalez♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔American Kidney Fund♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Kidney Fund asks why rural hospital closures continue while HHS prioritizes AI acceleration, as no algorithm replaces the nephrologist a Montana dialysis patient can no longer reach.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0032♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Lee♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Back in February my cardiologist in Greenville ran some computer program on my EKG and nobody told me what it was doing or who could see that data afterward. I found out by asking the nurse directly and she didnt even know. Im 65, I've done construction work for thirty years, and I need somebody looking me in the eye, not a printout nobody can explain. Wealthy patients will still get that. People like me will get handed off to a machine. You have a duty to protect us. Slow this down.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0033♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Hall♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔athenahealth♔♔♔♔♔♔Our organization supports the broader goal of AI adoption in clinical care, but the reimbursement infrastructure is not ready to support it at scale. Across our partner institutions, we tracked an average adjudication delay of 94 days per AI-assisted diagnostic claim, with payers applying eleven distinct eligibility frameworks, none aligned with current FDA guidance. Accelerating adoption before those inconsistencies are resolved means locking in a broken payment architecture that will be nearly impossible to unwind once scaled.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0034♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Taylor♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔Disability Rights Advocates♔♔♔♔♔♔Disability Rights Advocates urges HHS to require mandatory equity impact assessments before accelerating AI clinical adoption, as unprotected deployment will disproportionately harm disabled, rural, and low-income patients already failed by existing systems.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0035♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Mitchell♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To the Office of the Deputy Secretary:⏎⏎My sister Diane spent nearly two years receiving wrong diagnoses before a specialist finally slowed down and questioned what the labs were actually showing. What caught her condition was a human being willing to distrust the system. I think about that whenever I read about accelerating AI in clinical settings. The hospitals most likely to deploy these tools fastest are the ones with the thinnest staffing, and that means elderly patients, low-income patients, people with complicated histories like Diane's, are the ones most likely to receive care where the human second-guessing gets removed. HHS is supposed to protect people who face barriers, not build new ones for them. Please require equity impact reviews before expanding any of this.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Nelson♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔Optum♔♔♔♔♔♔Optum submits these comments with particular concern about a gap this RFI does not adequately address. Our organization operates across payer contract environments where AI-generated coverage determination logic is already producing adverse decisions at scale, and what happened with NaviHealth is not an isolated case. We work through an average of 340 staff hours per payer negotiation cycle resolving classification discrepancies that federal standards could otherwise eliminate, and we have watched that same administrative friction get weaponized against patients whose appeal burden is already unmanageable. Who exactly is reviewing these denials before they go out? Accelerating adoption without mandatory human review requirements for adverse determinations is a regulatory choice with identifiable victims, and HHS knows it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0037♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Hernandez♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony Hernandez, RN⏎Boise, Idaho⏎⏎May 12, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Forty-one years at the bedside teaches you something about what happens when you remove judgment from the loop. I have caught medication errors that computerized order systems flagged as acceptable. I have watched physicians override alerts so many times that the alerts stopped meaning anything. The problem was never the technology itself. The problem was the assumption that speed and automation are interchangeable with competence. What I am watching happen now in insurance coverage determinations is the same logic, applied to a part of the system where the patients are even less visible and even less able to fight back.⏎⏎The question HHS has asked in this RFI is essentially how to go faster. I want to ask a different one: faster toward what, and for whom?⏎⏎ProPublica and STAT News reported extensively on UnitedHealth Group's NaviHealth algorithm and what it was doing to Medicare Advantage patients who needed post-acute rehabilitation care. Patients who had physician recommendations in hand, patients whose doctors said they needed continued skilled nursing facility care, were being denied at rates that no clinician reviewing individual cases would have approved. A class action lawsuit was filed in 2023 alleging the algorithm was systematically overriding physician judgment. The denial rate wasn't an anomaly. It was the product.⏎⏎What gets me is the appeals data. Most patients who receive an AI-generated denial never appeal. Not because the denial was correct. Because the appeals process requires time, paperwork, English proficiency, a phone that works, and a family member who isn't already exhausted from providing care. Elderly patients don't have those things in abundance. Patients with disabilities don't. Low-income patients don't. Non-English speakers navigating a system that was never designed with them in mind certainly don't. So the denials disappear from the data. The algorithm looks like it's working. The harm is real and it is invisible by design, and I don't think that is an accident.⏎⏎I have been a nurse long enough to know that systems optimized for throughput tend to externalize their costs onto the people with the least power to resist. That is what is happening here.⏎⏎HHS has the authority to require that coverage determinations made using algorithmic tools receive meaningful human review before they become final. I am not talking about a checkbox. I mean a qualified person who has access to the actual clinical record, who has time to read it, and whose signature carries accountability. That is not an unreasonable ask. That is what we require of a nurse before she administers a medication.⏎⏎Choosing not to require that, while simultaneously publishing an initiative framed around acceleration, is a policy choice. It has consequences that are predictable and documented. The patients who will pay for that choice are the ones least equipped to survive an incorrect denial of post-acute care.⏎⏎I recognize HHS is not the only actor here. I recognize this is complicated. But the framing of this RFI treats human oversight as a potential obstacle to adoption, and I think that framing is wrong and I think you know it is wrong.⏎⏎The 84-year-old woman in the NaviHealth data who was denied her skilled nursing stay did not get a nurse standing next to her when the algorithm ran.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Anthony Hernandez, RN♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0038♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Smith♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔National Alliance for Caregiving♔♔♔♔♔♔National Alliance for Caregiving⏎1730 Rhode Island Avenue, NW, Suite 812⏎Washington, DC 20036⏎⏎June 3, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎To the Office of the Deputy Secretary:⏎⏎The National Alliance for Caregiving submits this comment with serious reservations about accelerating AI adoption in clinical care before democratic governance frameworks and meaningful community consent processes exist to guide those decisions responsibly.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎David Smith⏎Campaign Director⏎National Alliance for Caregiving⏎dsmith@caregiving.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0039♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Wilson♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My neighbor Dottie got misdiagnosed TWICE by one of those automated systems before a real nurse caught it. I have forty-one years at Vanderbilt and I genuinely cannot figure out what this rule actually DOES to patient coverage or who is responsible when the machine gets it wrong. Can someone please explain this in plain English!! I am not against technology but I keep thinking about who answers for Dottie when there is no human in the room checking.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔King♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔Zebra Medical Vision♔♔♔♔♔♔Zebra Medical Vision submits these comments with concern that accelerating AI adoption without preserving meaningful human accountability in clinical care risks converting patients into data points rather than people deserving genuine, answerable presence.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Perez♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist's office started using some kind of AI screening program back in January, and when it flagged something in my results, the nurse told me "the algorithm noted it" and genuinely could not say more than that. I spent three weeks frightened before a human doctor reviewed everything and told me the flag was almost certainly wrong for someone with my inflammation history. Nobody asked me first, and nobody seemed to think that was a problem.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0042♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Lopez♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔Infectious Diseases Society of America♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Infectious Diseases Society of America, IDSA respectfully submits that HHS must require mandatory bias auditing before any AI clinical decision support tool is deployed in safety-net settings serving underrepresented populations.⏎⏎Acceleration without equity safeguards is not progress.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0043♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Hill♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔American College of Emergency Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔The American College of Emergency Physicians urges HHS to pause AI acceleration and establish equity and informed-consent governance frameworks first, as 68% of member organizations report patient complaints tied to algorithmic decisions.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0044♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔White♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister spent three months getting bounced around after an automated tool flagged her wrong, and she ended up paying out of pocket for two procedures she didn't need before anyone actually looked at her full history. I run a small business, so i get it when people say speed and efficiency matter. they do. but what i keep thinking about is who pays when the system moves fast and gets it wrong. it's never the insurer.⏎⏎What I'm seeing more and more is insurers using AI to deny coverage before a human being has looked at anything. the UnitedHealth/NaviHealth situation that was in the news is the most visible version of this but it is not the only one. elderly patients, people with limited english, people who can't take a day off work to fight an appeal, they are the ones absorbing the cost of these errors. HHS has the authority to require that a real person sign off on every denial and that the appeals process be written in plain language and actually reachable by phone. not just technically available. I don't think that's a radical ask and i'd like to know why it hasn't happened yet.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0045♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔King♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MY DAUGHTER'S CARE AND THIS RULEMAKING⏎⏎My daughter Nora was misdiagnosed twice before a specialist in Spokane finally identified her autoimmune condition. What saved her was not a faster algorithm. It was a physician who stopped, questioned what the software was telling him, and ordered a panel the system had not flagged. I think about that moment a lot when I read about initiatives to accelerate AI in clinical settings, because what I hear in the word "accelerate" is an assumption that speed is the problem we're solving. I'm not sure that's right.⏎⏎I'll be honest: my pharmacist mentioned something about new transparency requirements being part of this, and I don't fully understand whether that's what this request for information is addressing. I'm a stay-at-home parent, not a health policy professional. But I've spent the past two years learning a lot I didn't want to have to learn, and I have some things I want to say.⏎⏎ON ACCOUNTABILITY AND THE HUMAN IN THE CHAIN⏎⏎Nora's records now live in systems I have no real visibility into. Her history of those two misdiagnoses, her genetic markers, the notes from the specialist who finally got it right. I don't know who can see that information, what software is running analyses on it, or whether any AI-generated output based on her file would carry a label that said so. That last part matters to me more than almost anything else in this discussion.⏎⏎When a physician makes an error, there is a legal and ethical accountability structure. It is imperfect, but it exists. When an algorithm generates a recommendation that leads to harm, I genuinely do not know who is responsible. I don't think most patients know either. HHS has existing civil rights obligations under Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act, and the Office for Civil Rights has issued guidance on nondiscrimination in health programs. My reading of that framework is that deploying AI tools without first assessing whether they produce disparate outcomes for different patient populations is inconsistent with what those obligations already require. I'm not a lawyer, but that seems like a straightforward reading.⏎⏎ON EQUITY AND WHO BEARS THE RISK⏎⏎What worries me is that the patients least able to push back are the ones most likely to encounter algorithmic decision-making with the least oversight. Patients in under-resourced clinics, patients who don't speak English as a first language, patients on Medicaid who may see a provider for twelve minutes. If an AI tool has been validated primarily on data from well-resourced health systems, its outputs in those settings are not guaranteed to be reliable. I don't think that's a controversial claim. I just don't see where the safeguard is.⏎⏎A SPECIFIC REQUEST⏎⏎I'm asking HHS to condition any AI acceleration initiative on mandatory equity impact assessments and independent civil rights review before these tools are deployed in clinical settings that serve Medicaid, Medicare, or other publicly funded patient populations. Not as a checkbox. As a real prerequisite, with findings that are public and reviewable.⏎⏎Nora is doing better now. But I keep thinking about the families who don't have the same access to a specialist in Spokane, and whether the algorithm those families encounter has ever been tested on anyone who looks like them or lives where they do.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0046♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Smith♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔GE HealthCare♔♔♔♔♔♔GE HealthCare Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎REIMBURSEMENT ALIGNMENT⏎⏎Inconsistent payer certification standards remain the primary barrier our deployment partners identify. Averaging 4.3 separate certification processes per tool adds roughly seven months to every product cycle.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Linda Smith⏎Compliance Officer, GE HealthCare♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0047♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Martin♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MY CONCERN ABOUT WHO IS ACTUALLY RESPONSIBLE⏎⏎My cardiologist at a clinic in Jackson told me they are starting to use a computer program to read EKGs, and when I asked him point blank who answers for it if the machine gets something wrong, he went quiet for a second before saying the doctor is still in charge. I have heard that before. I spent 22 years in the Army and I learned that "someone is in charge" and "someone is watching out for you" are not the same thing, and the distance between those two ideas is where people get hurt.⏎⏎What worries me is not whether the program is accurate most of the time. What worries me is that medicine depends on a real human being in the room who feels responsible for you personally, not just professionally, and I do not know how that survives when a machine is doing part of the thinking. My ask to HHS is simple and I mean it plainly: before you move faster on any of this, make sure you can answer who that person is for every patient, and make sure they know it too.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0048♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Campbell♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Anxiety and Depression Association of America♔♔♔♔♔♔The Anxiety and Depression Association of America urges HHS to pause and build real governance guardrails before accelerating AI adoption in clinical behavioral health settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0049♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Adams♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔American Liver Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Liver Foundation, we urge HHS to require equity impact assessments before accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings, as vulnerable patients face the greatest risk from unvalidated algorithmic tools.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0050♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Jackson♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My granddaughter Renee was misdiagnosed twice before a doctor at Grady Hospital finally sat down with her full history and caught what every screening had missed. Please do not rush this.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0051♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Martin♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist retired, and the new doctor spent four minutes with me before an algorithm apparently decided I was low-risk. It took my wife refusing to leave the office before anyone actually listened to my chest with a stethoscope and caught the arrhythmia the software had flagged as not worth following up on. I'm not telling that story to complain about one doctor. I'm telling it because I want HHS to understand what "acceleration" looks like from the patient's end of the table.⏎⏎Forty years in construction. I have watched foremen skip safety protocols because the schedule was tight and the system said the numbers were acceptable. Some of those men are dead. The logic that gets workers killed on job sites is exactly the same logic driving AI-based coverage denials right now: trust the model, move fast, and assume the errors are small enough to absorb.⏎⏎They are not small enough to absorb.⏎⏎ProPublica and STAT News reported in detail on UnitedHealth Group's NaviHealth nH Predict algorithm, which was generating denials for post-acute care coverage for Medicare Advantage patients at rates that bore almost no relationship to what human reviewers would have decided. Elderly patients, people recovering from strokes and hip fractures, being cut off from rehab coverage because a model said statistically they should be done by now. That reporting described internal targets, physicians overriding their own clinical judgment because the algorithm said otherwise, and patients who simply didn't have the energy or the family support to fight back.⏎⏎Those are the people bearing the cost of this.⏎⏎When HHS asks what actions it can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, I want to be direct about what I'm hearing in that question. Acceleration without mandatory human review requirements is a policy choice. It is not a neutral stance. HHS knows the NaviHealth situation exists. The reporting is public. The lawsuits are public. Deciding to push AI adoption faster while not requiring that a qualified human clinician document their review of any coverage denial is a decision HHS is making on behalf of the patients who cannot fight back.⏎⏎I am not opposed to AI in medicine. I genuinely think there are places where it probably helps. But there is a difference between a tool that assists a doctor and a system that replaces the doctor's judgment on whether a 71-year-old stroke patient gets three more weeks of rehabilitation. The second one needs a human being's name attached to it. A real clinician who reviewed the case and is accountable for the outcome.⏎⏎What I want HHS to say explicitly, in whatever guidance comes out of this process, is this:⏎⏎1. No AI-generated coverage determination is final without documented human clinical review by a licensed clinician.⏎2. That review must be documented in a way the patient or their family can request and receive.⏎3. Denials that cannot show that documentation are presumptively invalid.⏎⏎That is not a complicated ask. It is the minimum standard I would expect on any job site before someone signs off that a structure is safe to occupy. You don't let the software make the final call when people's lives are on the line. You require a human signature and you make that person answerable for what they signed.⏎⏎HHS has the authority to require this right now. The question is whether it will.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0052♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Nguyen♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas Nguyen⏎47 Birchwood Lane⏎Hamden, CT 06514⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎⏎TO WHOM IT MAY CONCERN:⏎⏎My wife went through a breast cancer scare two years back. The imaging center had just rolled out some automated flagging system and for three weeks we got contradictory information that nobody at that office could really explain. I still don't know whether the software got it right or the radiologist did. That kind of uncertainty, sitting in a waiting room not knowing, stays with you. Medicine is supposed to be a person who knows you and is answerable to you. Whatever you decide here, please slow down and put that first.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Thomas Nguyen♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0053♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Scott♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔eClinicalWorks♔♔♔♔♔♔eClinicalWorks submits these comments with serious concern that accelerating AI adoption without resolving payer liability standards will cost hospitals like ours hundreds of thousands annually while eroding the human judgment that medicine requires.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0054♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Campbell♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔American Occupational Therapy Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Occupational Therapy Association urges HHS to require mandatory equity impact assessments and disparity monitoring as preconditions for any accelerated AI deployment pathway, not afterthoughts appended once harm is already documented.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Perez♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist's practice switched systems two years ago, and an algorithm classified my echocardiogram results as low priority because, I later found out, the model had been trained predominantly on data from men under sixty-five. Eleven days passed before a physician actually looked at my file. By then the atrial irregularity they found required immediate intervention. I am not telling this story to be dramatic. I am telling it because that system was making a clinical judgment about me, and nobody in that office knew to question it.⏎⏎What I want to say in this comment is narrower than the full scope of what HHS is asking. I keep coming back to one thing: what happens when these same systems get embedded in insurance coverage determinations, and nobody is required to explain to a patient why the algorithm said no.⏎⏎The investigative reporting by ProPublica and STAT News on UnitedHealth Group's NaviHealth algorithm is public record. That system was denying post-acute care to Medicare Advantage patients at rates that far exceeded what their own physicians recommended. Elderly patients recovering from surgery were being discharged weeks earlier than their doctors thought was safe, because a predictive model said they should be. When families tried to appeal, they were fighting a process that gave them no real explanation of how the denial decision was made, and no genuine path to challenge it.⏎⏎This is where I think HHS needs to be direct with itself about what acceleration means in practice. If the Department moves faster on AI adoption in clinical and insurance settings without first establishing enforceable explainability standards, the patients who will bear the cost of that speed are not randomly distributed. They are:⏎⏎1. Elderly patients, like me, whose demographic characteristics are underrepresented in many training datasets.⏎2. Disabled patients who depend on post-acute and long-term care coverage and have the least capacity to absorb a wrongful denial while they fight through an appeal.⏎3. Low-income patients who cannot afford to hire anyone to help them navigate an appeals process that was designed when a human being made the initial decision and could at least be asked to explain their reasoning.⏎4. Non-English-speaking patients for whom even the existing appeals infrastructure is already nearly inaccessible.⏎⏎When an AI system issues a coverage denial, the patient needs to know what inputs drove that decision. That is not a technical impossibility. It is a policy choice whether to require it.⏎⏎HHS has authority under existing law to condition participation in federal programs on meaningful consumer protections. Choosing not to use that authority while simultaneously pushing for faster AI adoption is itself a decision. It is not a neutral default. There will be patients harmed by denials that a human reviewer would have caught or overturned, and if the Department has accelerated the deployment of these systems without requiring mandatory human review of AI-generated coverage denials and enforceable appeal rights, then HHS will have known that outcome was foreseeable.⏎⏎My specific ask is this: before any further acceleration of AI in insurance functions, require that every AI-generated coverage denial include a plain-language explanation of the basis for the decision, trigger an automatic right to human review upon request, and that the human reviewer have actual authority to override the algorithm.⏎⏎I would also genuinely like to know whether there is a public hearing scheduled for this rulemaking where I could speak in person.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0056♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Hill♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔Partnership for Health Analytic Research♔♔♔♔♔♔Partnership for Health Analytic Research | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎- - -⏎⏎June 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: Request for Information, Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎On behalf of the Partnership for Health Analytic Research, a network of 1,200 licensed psychotherapists and mental health advocates, we urge HHS to reorient this effort toward accountability before acceleration.⏎⏎Partnership for Health Analytic Research | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎Our 2023 membership survey found that 84% of members expressed serious concern about algorithmic tools in clinical decision-making, particularly for patients from historically marginalized communities. Our policy review committee documented 47 instances in which algorithmic recommendations appeared to reflect racially or socioeconomically biased assumptions, producing delayed or inappropriate care referrals. These are not edge cases.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI troubles us. HHS asks how to accelerate adoption, not whether the structural conditions for responsible adoption exist. Hospitals and insurers have clear financial incentives to deploy AI primarily as a cost-cutting mechanism, reducing clinical headcount and expanding caseloads, rather than improving care. Our members report that poorly implemented tools increase documentation burden while stripping clinicians of the institutional standing needed to override recommendations they know to be wrong. The workers bearing these costs are disproportionately women and workers of color. We genuinely do not understand why HHS would propose accelerating deployment before addressing who captures the benefits of these systems and who absorbs the harm.⏎⏎HHS should condition any acceleration framework on mandatory disclosure requirements, meaningful opt-out protections for patients, and enforceable worker protections for clinicians whose professional judgment is displaced by automated workflows.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Susan Hill⏎Executive Director, Partnership for Health Analytic Research⏎contact@pharesearch.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0057♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Perez♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister spent TWO YEARS getting bounced between doctors before anyone figured out she had lupus. She's a Latina woman and I know that mattered. Now HHS wants to speed all this up with AI and I genuinely don't understand how that helps her. What those doctors missed wasn't information, it was willingness to actually look at her. A doctor reads something in a patient's face, in how they're sitting, in what they won't say out loud. No screen catches that. Faster diagnosis means nothing if the same blind spots just run quicker. Please slow down before you optimize that relationship away entirely.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0058♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Campbell♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔American Psychological Association♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Psychological Association urges HHS to ensure that accelerating AI adoption in clinical care never diminishes the irreplaceable human presence patients depend on when they are scared or in pain.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0059♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Green♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To Whom It May Concern at HHS,⏎⏎My granddaughter Lily was misdiagnosed twice before a nurse practitioner in Casper, Wyoming sat with two years of her medical records for nearly two hours and noticed a pattern that finally led to her autoimmune diagnosis. No algorithm flagged it. A careful human being did. I have spent forty years in social work watching what happens when systems prioritize speed over that kind of slow, attentive judgment, and the word "accelerate" in this request genuinely worries me. The patients who suffer most when care gets depersonalized are always the ones with the least power to push back: rural patients, patients whose symptoms don't match whatever profile the system was trained on, patients who can't easily get a second opinion. There are a lot of Lilys out there. Please require strong human oversight before moving forward, and do not let efficiency metrics drive decisions that belong to a person who is accountable to the patient sitting in front of them.⏎⏎Respectfully,⏎Amanda Green⏎Sheridan, Wyoming♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Miller♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔Society of Critical Care Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The Society of Critical Care Medicine urges HHS to require mandatory human-relationship impact assessments before any AI clinical tool reaches patients in behavioral health or underserved settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0061♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Scott♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist overrode a diagnostic algorithm last year and I genuinely believe that override is the reason I'm sitting here writing this comment instead of dealing with a second cardiac event. The software flagged me as low-risk. She disagreed because she knows my family history and she knows that my fatigue doesn't present the way the textbook says it should. She made a call the algorithm wasn't equipped to make, and she was right. I'm not a physician. I work in a warehouse in Tacoma, have for nineteen years. But I understand systems. I understand what happens when you remove a human checkpoint to speed up throughput. The errors don't disappear. They just stop getting caught. That's not an opinion, that's how supply chains fail. You optimize out the inspection step and the bad inventory ships. Same logic.⏎⏎What worries me about this rulemaking is that HHS seems to be treating the pace of adoption as the problem to solve. I'd argue the accountability gap is the problem, and accelerating adoption before you close that gap is exactly backwards. Right now if an AI system contributes to a clinical error, the liability chain looks something like this: the clinician who relied on it, the hospital that deployed it, the vendor that sold it, the company that built the training data set. Each one of those handoffs is a designed escape hatch. I came across a piece in JAMA from 2019, W. Nicholson Price and colleagues, making essentially this point about how malpractice doctrine doesn't cleanly assign liability when AI is in the mix. That was 2019. I don't think the legal situation has gotten cleaner since then.⏎⏎The FDA clearance question bothers me too. A device can be cleared as a medical device and still perform completely differently in a specific hospital's patient population. Clearance isn't a guarantee of safety in deployment, it's more like a starting point, and treating it as a finish line is a kind of false assurance that I think is genuinely dangerous.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the governance question in this RFI because I think HHS is framing it wrong. The question seems to be: what administrative hurdles are slowing down AI adoption? My answer is that the real hurdle isn't administrative friction, it's the absence of a legally enforceable accountability structure. Before you accelerate anything, someone needs to be identifiably and legally responsible when an AI-assisted recommendation causes harm. Not theoretically responsible. Actually responsible, in a way that a patient or a patient's family can pursue.⏎⏎I also want to say, and I realize this is a tangent, who actually reads these comments. I've submitted three public comments over the years on different rules and I have never once received any indication that the substance was considered. I don't know if this goes into a database somewhere or if a staffer reads it. I'm writing it anyway because my cardiologist made a judgment call that the algorithm couldn't make, and somebody at HHS should know that before they decide the algorithm♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0062♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Williams♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔Intelerad Medical Systems♔♔♔♔♔♔Intelerad Medical Systems submits these comments with some reluctance, because the honest answer to HHS's question about accelerating AI adoption is that our network is not ready, and we suspect many others are not either. Our Q2 2024 compliance review found that 68% of the patient-facing digital health tools we were actively using could not be cleanly classified under existing regulatory or reimbursement frameworks, and our administrative staff spent an average of 14 hours per month per tool trying to reconcile payer documentation requirements that varied across just three regional insurers. That cost us approximately $42,000 in unreimbursed administrative overhead in a single fiscal year, and that number does not reflect any AI tools that were actually sophisticated enough to raise clinical governance concerns. What it reflects is baseline classification ambiguity that nobody has resolved. Accelerating adoption into that environment does not reduce complexity; it locks the complexity in, because once these tools are embedded in reimbursement codes and clinical workflows, no compliance officer and no regulator will have a practical mechanism to remove them. HHS should establish classification standards, audit requirements, and accountability structures before it asks health systems to move faster.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0063♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Johnson♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My client Marcus, a 34-year-old Cherokee man I'd been working with for two years, got flagged as low-risk by some automated screening tool his Medicaid plan was using and got routed to a self-guided app instead of an actual person, and three weeks later he was in the emergency room after a crisis that I could have told anyone was coming if they'd bothered to ask me and I'm a social worker in Tulsa not a clinician with fancy credentials just someone who knew him and the thing is Marcus didn't communicate his distress in ways a checklist would catch he went quiet he stopped returning texts he made a joke the last time we talked that wasn't really a joke and no algorithm was going to read that and I don't know what HHS thinks it's accelerating here but whatever efficiency gain got calculated somebody should also calculate what it cost Marcus and whether the people who built that tool will ever have to find out.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Hill♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister Renee spent eight months being told she was fine by a clinic outside Scranton before a human specialist finally caught what was actually wrong, and I think about her every time I read about HHS wanting to speed AI adoption up. What worries me is the EHR situation all over again — hospitals got locked into expensive systems they can't leave because the switching costs are too high, and I'm genuinely afraid we're about to do that same thing, faster, with patient diagnoses on the line.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0065♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Brown♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔AARP♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of AARP Massachusetts, we urge HHS to require mandatory bias auditing, independent oversight, and equity impact assessments before any acceleration of AI deployment in clinical settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0066♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Gonzalez♔♔New York♔♔United States♔Arthritis Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the Arthritis Foundation, this comment responds to HHS's request regarding AI acceleration in clinical care.⏎⏎The Arthritis Foundation's members, many on fixed incomes, face a direct question this RFI does not answer: will AI-assisted care cost them more at the point of service? That concern is not abstract. Consider the following:⏎⏎1. Reimbursement structures for AI-assisted visits remain undefined.⏎2. Cost-sharing implications for algorithmic tools are unaddressed.⏎3. Low-income patients have no guaranteed right to opt out without penalty.⏎⏎HHS must clarify these reimbursement questions before accelerating deployment further.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0067♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Lee♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother's oncologist at Montefiore told us, sometime in early 2023, that a machine-learning tool had flagged something in her imaging that had nearly been missed. She got her biopsy two weeks sooner than she might have. I am genuinely grateful for that. But when we asked how the tool worked, what it was weighing, whether her age or her Caribbean background had factored in somehow, nobody in that room could tell us. Not because they were hiding anything. Just because nobody knew. That silence has stayed with me, and it is why I am submitting this comment.⏎⏎What I want to say, as plainly as I can, is that I have watched this pattern before. Not with AI, but with electronic health records. With clinical decision support alerts. The promise was real, the rollouts were rushed, and what we got was physicians clicking through dozens of irrelevant warnings per shift just to get to the one that mattered. Sepsis prediction algorithms that performed well in published studies and then missed patients in real hospitals where the data was incomplete, coded differently, or just absent. I am not a clinician or an engineer, but I read carefully, and the history of health IT is not a story of bad intentions. It is a story of tools that worked in controlled conditions and then met real clinical environments, which are nothing like controlled conditions.⏎⏎This is the part I keep thinking about. The records that feed these AI tools in actual hospitals are fragmented. A patient transferred from another system brings records that may be incomplete, inconsistently coded, or missing entire medication histories. A patient who stopped taking a medication six months ago but never told anyone. The training data for most of these tools does not look like that. Researchers who have studied this, including work published by Obermeyer and colleagues, have documented how predictably clinical AI performance degrades when tools move from the populations they were trained on to the populations they actually encounter. This is not an edge case. It is the norm.⏎⏎And then there is something I want to explain carefully, because I think it matters more than it gets credit for. When clinicians use an AI tool and it is usually right, they start to trust it the way you trust a colleague who has a good track record. That is human. That is reasonable. But it means that when the tool is wrong, the clinician may not be looking as hard as they would have been without the tool. The moments when the AI is most likely to fail, a patient who doesn't match the training population, a situation that changed in the last 48 hours, are exactly the moments when that reduced vigilance is most dangerous. The tool cannot tell you it is operating outside its reliable range. It just gives you an answer.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to accelerate adoption, I feel two things at once. I want that for my mother and for everyone else's mother. And I am worried that "accelerate" means skipping the part where we actually understand where these tools fail before we let them into consequential decisions.⏎⏎The question this RFI keeps circling around is whether regulation is a barrier to innovation. I think that framing gets it backwards. The barrier is the gap between how well these tools work in demonstrations and how well they work on real patients with real records in real hospitals. If we rush past that gap and the tools fail visibly and publicly, we will have damaged public trust in something that could genuinely help people.⏎⏎My mother's oncologist still can't tell me how that algorithm worked.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0068♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Allen♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: Re: HHS AI comment submission -- Kenneth Allen⏎⏎---⏎⏎I followed a link from Facebook and I am honestly not sure I ended up in the right place, but I am going to say what I came to say. I had a patient, Edna, 71 years old, drove nearly three hours from Pine Ridge, and a computer flag nearly sent her down the wrong treatment path because it did not know her history or what her traditional healer had her on. I caught it because I know her. Someone who knows the patient has to have the final word. Always.⏎⏎-- Kenneth Allen, RN⏎Sent from my phone♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0069♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Perez♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔American College of Preventive Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔American College of Preventive Medicine⏎1307 New York Avenue NW, Suite 200⏎Washington, DC 20005⏎⏎May 12, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎The American College of Preventive Medicine urges HHS to establish clear, non-waivable accountability structures before accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings where diffused liability leaves harmed patients without recourse.⏎⏎Michelle Perez⏎Senior Policy Analyst⏎American College of Preventive Medicine⏎mperez@acpm.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0070♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Sanchez♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My bypass surgery was in 2009, at a hospital in Henderson. I have kept every record since then in a binder — medication changes, dosing histories, physician notes. Two years ago, when my cardiologist retired and I spent eight months being shuffled between three different doctors at the same practice, not one of them had read my full history before adjusting my medications. I caught a dosing error myself, by comparing a new prescription against my own notes. That experience is why I am paying close attention to what HHS is proposing here.⏎⏎THE QUESTION BEHIND THE QUESTION⏎⏎The RFI asks how HHS can accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. I want to respectfully suggest that this is the wrong starting question. The prior question is who gets to decide how much artificial intelligence belongs in medical decisions, and what happens when that decision cannot be undone.⏎⏎I am not opposed to AI in healthcare. I have seen how fragmented records and overworked physicians produce exactly the kind of error I described above, and I understand why people believe better technology could help. But I keep coming back to something that health informatics researchers have documented for years: once a clinical technology is embedded in billing codes, workflow systems, and reimbursement structures, it does not get removed. Sittig and Singh wrote about this in 2012 in the context of electronic health records, and the pattern has only become more entrenched since then. Health IT systems get institutionalized, and then the political and financial cost of reversing them exceeds the cost of tolerating their known failures. That is the world we are already living in with EHRs, and it is the world we will be living in with AI if HHS uses its regulatory and reimbursement levers to lock in adoption before governance exists.⏎⏎WHAT CONCERNS ME SPECIFICALLY⏎⏎I want to address two things from the RFI, not everything in it:⏎⏎1. Reimbursement as acceleration. If Medicare and Medicaid reimbursement is structured to reward AI-assisted clinical decisions before independent oversight frameworks exist, health systems will adopt those tools because the financial incentives require it. That is not a choice. That is a mandate disguised as a market signal.⏎⏎2. The absence of a democratic process. How much AI should participate in a diagnosis, a treatment recommendation, a medication adjustment? That is a moral question. It belongs to public deliberation, not to an agency acting under industry-aligned urgency. I am 74 years old and I have watched enough policy get made in my lifetime to know that "we will fix the governance later" is how permanent decisions get made without accountability.⏎⏎The ratchet only turns one direction. Once AI is embedded in clinical workflows and reimbursement codes, the political cost of removing it will be higher than the cost of tolerating whatever harms it produces. We have already learned this lesson. I am asking HHS not to repeat it at a larger scale.⏎⏎MY ASK⏎⏎HHS should pause the acceleration posture. Convene genuine public deliberation before using reimbursement levers to institutionalize this technology. Establish independent oversight with real authority, not advisory boards. And be honest about the fact that the decisions being made now are not temporary pilots. They are the foundation of a system that future patients, including patients who are children today, will not be able to change.⏎⏎I caught my own dosing error because I kept paper records. Future patients may not have that option if the system is designed around tools they cannot audit or question.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0071♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Hall♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center♔♔♔♔♔♔University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center⏎Department of Health Policy⏎5323 Harry Hines Boulevard, Dallas, TX 75390⏎⏎June 2, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎To the Office of the Deputy Secretary:⏎⏎The University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center's health policy faculty respectfully submits these comments in response to the Department's RFI on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. Medicine is a relational practice grounded in moral accountability, and HHS should resist framing AI adoption as straightforwardly beneficial without accounting for what is lost when algorithmic intermediaries displace the clinician-patient relationship.⏎⏎David Hall⏎Professor of Health Policy⏎UT Southwestern Medical Center♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0072♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Anderson♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔Epilepsy Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔The Epilepsy Foundation calls on HHS to require mandatory human review of all AI-driven coverage denials before any further acceleration of automated insurance functions harming vulnerable patients.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0073♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Gonzalez♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔National Disability Rights Network♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Disability Rights Network, we ask HHS to pause and reconsider the framing of this RFI. Our 2024 member survey across 847 dental practices found that 63 percent of sites serving FQHC populations lacked adequate informed-consent protocols when deploying AI diagnostic tools with limited-English-proficiency patients.⏎⏎No acceleration framework should move forward without mandatory pre-deployment equity audits, post-deployment disparity monitoring, and enforceable standards governing AI tools used in insurance determinations for underserved populations.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0074♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Williams♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist's practice got a new doctor about two years back, and during my first visit he told me the system was recommending a particular medication based on my profile. I asked him what that meant. He couldn't tell me. Just said the algorithm flagged it. I have that prescription sitting in a drawer at home, still unfilled. That's not a technology problem, that's a trust problem, and I don't think HHS is taking it seriously enough.⏎⏎What I keep thinking about is my own industry. I've worked in warehouses for over thirty years, and I watched management bring in automated systems one after another. Every time, they said it would make things better for workers and customers. What it actually did was let them cut staff and call it efficiency. The people who lost those jobs, in warehouses and now in hospital billing offices, are mostly women, a lot of them minority women. I don't see why healthcare AI is going to be different. Before this moves forward, somebody at HHS ought to ask a simple question: if a frontline doctor can't explain or override what the machine is telling them, who exactly is accountable when something goes wrong.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0075♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Anderson♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Caitlin was misdiagnosed twice at UPMC before she was sixteen. An algorithm flagged her arrhythmia as low-risk. Her pediatrician cited the software as if that settled it. A human cardiologist eventually caught what the system missed. I think about that a lot.⏎⏎The training data feeding that tool was drawn predominantly from adult male patients. This is not a fringe concern I am raising — Obermeyer and colleagues documented in Science in 2019 exactly how this kind of bias gets baked into clinical algorithms and then systematically produces worse outcomes for patients who don't look like the training population. My daughter was sixteen. She did not look like the training population.⏎⏎I want to say something about the structure of what HHS is asking in this RFI, because the framing bothers me as much as anything else here. The questions read like the decision to accelerate has already been made and you are asking for input on implementation. That is not democratic governance. That is asking the public to help you do something you have already decided to do.⏎⏎The irreversibility question is the one I can't get past. No major health system has successfully ripped out an embedded EHR once it is woven into workflows and billing. Not one. The technology becomes load-bearing. The same thing will happen here, faster, because you are talking about tying AI tools to reimbursement codes. Once a clinical AI product is attached to how a hospital gets paid, the political cost of removing it will always exceed the political cost of tolerating whatever harm it causes. Future policymakers will inherit a system they cannot undo, and they will know it, and they will leave it in place.⏎⏎That is the ratchet effect. And HHS is being asked to set it in motion on a timeline that reads like it was written by the vendors who stand to profit from it.⏎⏎I teach elementary school. I spend thirty years telling children to question their sources and to think about who benefits from a particular answer. I am genuinely, specifically furious that the adults responsible for my child's care had been quietly trained to defer to software output without asking who built it, on what data, and with what incentives.⏎⏎A democratic society is entitled to decide that medicine should be slower, more accountable, and more human, even if that costs some efficiency. That is a values choice. It belongs to the public. It does not belong to an administrative agency responding on an industry timeline through an RFI process.⏎⏎My specific ask is this: HHS should not attach reimbursement incentives to AI clinical tools until independent governance frameworks exist, built with real public input, not just comment periods most people don't know are happening.⏎⏎I found out about this one because a colleague forwarded me a link two days before the deadline.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Flores♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔Aetna♔♔♔♔♔♔Aetna Comments — HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎The Department has framed this request for information around accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, and that framing, while understandable, risks obscuring a more immediate problem that our organization has watched compound across multiple policy cycles without adequate federal response. The efficiency gains HHS envisions in care delivery are real and worth pursuing. But efficiency gains in coverage denial processing are not the same thing, and treating them as equivalent is, in our assessment, the central analytical error running through this RFI.⏎⏎What I keep returning to, professionally, is how much of the current regulatory uncertainty around insurance AI tools is not actually a patient protection problem in isolation. It is also a market structure problem. When there are no enforceable human-review standards or explainability requirements for AI-generated organization determinations, plans and vendors operate in a compliance vacuum that generates its own overhead. Our compliance staff cannot evaluate vendor tools against a standard that does not exist. That is not a complaint about overregulation; it is the opposite.⏎⏎The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations released its report on NaviHealth and UnitedHealth in October 2024, documenting that the nH Predict algorithm was used to generate post-acute care denials at rates that bore no discernible relationship to individualized clinical review (U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations, 2024). That is a documented, evidentiary record, not an allegation.⏎⏎Aetna Comments — HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎KFF's prior authorization analysis from March 2023 found that denial burdens fall disproportionately on low-income and disabled Medicare Advantage enrollees, who have the least administrative capacity to navigate appeals (Koma et al., 2023). The AMA's 2023 physician survey found that 94% of respondents reported care delays attributable to prior authorization, with 33% linking a serious adverse event to a denial. These are foreseeable harms. HHS possesses existing authority under 42 C.F.R. § 422 and the ACA's nondiscrimination provisions to act on them without waiting for new legislation.⏎⏎Obermeyer and Topol wrote in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2023 about systemic error propagation when AI tools operate without individualized clinical review — the monoculture problem, where a single flawed algorithm scales its errors across millions of coverage determinations simultaneously. That is the regulatory gap this RFI does not address.⏎⏎What HHS should require, specifically: meaningful human review of all AI-generated coverage denials, not as a checkbox but as a documented, auditable process; explainability disclosures to patients and ordering providers at the point of denial; and enforceable appeal rights with burden-shifting to the insurer when AI tools are the basis for an organization determination. The CMS Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule from January 2024 (CMS-0057-F) already established infrastructure on which these requirements could be built.⏎⏎The Department will hear a great deal in this docket about clinical AI's promise. That case is not difficult to make. What is more difficult, apparently, is acknowledging that the same technology deployed in utilization management contexts requires a different and more skeptical regulatory posture, and that the statutory authority to require one already exists on the shelf in♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0077♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Moore♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔Stanford University School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Stanford University School of Medicine respectfully submits that HHS must condition AI adoption investment on mandatory pre-deployment bias auditing, demographic performance stratification, and post-market surveillance as threshold requirements, not optional guidance.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0078♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Nelson♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔National Community Pharmacists Association♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the National Community Pharmacists Association, we urge HHS to condition any acceleration of clinical AI adoption on mandatory bias auditing requirements. Our 2023 membership survey found safety-net facilities serving Medicaid populations were 43% less likely to have formal algorithmic validation procedures in place.⏎⏎Documented harms like pulse oximetry failures in patients with darker skin tones are not hypothetical. Deploying AI at scale before equity-stratified validation standards exist will embed those disparities permanently.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0079♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Lewis♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My buddy Dave spent eight months fighting an automated system that flagged him low priority for pain management because somebody coded his service records wrong at one point, and nobody could tell him why the computer decided what it decided or who he was supposed to appeal to. That's not a technology problem in isolation. That's what happens when you build something that looks confident on top of data that isn't. VA records are incomplete, fragmented across facilities, full of gaps nobody bothered to fix. Any AI reading those records doesn't know what it doesn't know, and neither does the clinician trusting it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Johnson♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔American Association of Naturopathic Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Association of Naturopathic Physicians, we urge HHS to treat AI adoption in clinical care as a labor and equity issue, not merely a technology deployment question, before accelerating further implementation.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0081♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Brown♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔BenevolentAI♔♔♔♔♔♔BenevolentAI Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 1⏎⏎Submitted to: U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎BenevolentAI appreciates the Department's attention to the structural conditions necessary for responsible AI deployment in clinical settings. Our organization operates across multiple payer environments and has documented, through systematic partner analysis, that 67% of health technology organizations identify payer inconsistency as their primary barrier to scaling AI-assisted clinical tools, while 41% cite unresolved liability frameworks as a direct deterrent to entering new clinical workflow segments. We raise these figures not to argue against adoption broadly, but to indicate that the accountability infrastructure is not ready to support the pace HHS appears to be contemplating.⏎⏎ON THE SEQUENCING PROBLEM⏎⏎The Department's framing treats accelerated adoption as the policy objective, with accountability measures presumably to follow. This sequencing is the problem. Once AI systems are embedded in EHR workflows and reimbursement structures, they become practically irreversible — hospitals will have contracted around them, clinicians will have built documentation habits through them, and payers will have adjusted coding expectations to them. Known harms, when they emerge, will be absorbed rather than corrected because the cost of removal will exceed the cost of tolerance. This is not a hypothetical concern. It is the predictable consequence of deploying technology before establishing who bears legal responsibility when the technology fails.⏎⏎ON LIABILITY INCOHERENCE⏎⏎Mello et al. (2023), writing in the Journal of Health Politics, Policy and Law, documented what they called the "accountability gap" in AI-assisted clinical harm: clinicians invoke the learned intermediary doctrine, hospitals point to vendor indemnification clauses, and vendors disclaim liability through contract terms that, as ONC's 2019 issue brief on EHR contracts documented, health systems have almost no bargaining power to modify. Obermeyer and Topol (2023) in the New England Journal of Medicine noted that this liability diffusion is not incidental — it is structurally embedded in how AI products are currently brought to market.⏎⏎BenevolentAI Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 2⏎⏎FDA 510(k) clearance does not resolve this. The FDA's own January 2021 AI/ML SaMD Action Plan acknowledged that substantial equivalence review evaluates whether a device is similar to a predicate, not whether it performs safely in specific deployment environments. A cleared algorithm embedded in a clinical decision support workflow at a rural Alaska hospital is not the same product that was evaluated during clearance. The American Hospital Association's March 2024 advisory on vendor contract terms confirmed that most health system legal counsel cannot identify a solvent defendant in AI-related adverse event scenarios.⏎⏎ON WHAT HHS SHOULD DO INSTEAD⏎⏎HHS should condition any reimbursement incentive or adoption acceleration program on the prior establishment of mandatory vendor indemnification floors, explicit liability allocation rules that survive contractual disclaimers, and post-market surveillance requirements that are operationally independent of FDA clearance status. Without these conditions, acceleration does not reduce harm —⏎⏎it just redistributes it away from parties with resources to bear it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0082♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Brown♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My neurologist mentioned it almost in passing, the way you'd mention that the lab results were already in the system. She said an AI tool had flagged something in my imaging before she'd even pulled it up herself. I sat there for a second trying to figure out how I felt about that. Relieved, yes. But also like something had happened to me that I hadn't agreed to, and couldn't quite name.⏎⏎I'm 59 years old, two decades of service, three deployments. The VA in Portland is the only medical system I've really learned to navigate, and I've done it without family close by, without a civilian primary care doctor who understands what blast exposure does to the brain over time. My neurologist knows my history. She knows me. When she reviews my imaging, that relationship is part of the diagnostic process, not just background noise.⏎⏎I keep coming back to a research review I read once, Kelley and colleagues published it in PLOS ONE in 2014, that found the patient-clinician relationship had measurable effects on health outcomes independent of whatever technical intervention was used. Meaning the relationship itself is doing clinical work. That's not a soft finding. That's something that should be weighing heavily on whoever is designing these systems.⏎⏎What I want HHS to understand is this: the moment the AI reviewed my scan before my doctor did, the sequence of care changed. Whether the outcome was better or worse isn't really the point. The point is that I didn't know it was happening, I hadn't consented to it, and I have no idea what population that algorithm was trained on or whether women like me, older women with atypical neurological presentation profiles from blast exposure, were anywhere in that dataset.⏎⏎I'm not opposed to AI in medicine. I want to be clear about that. If a tool catches something my doctor might have missed at 6pm on a Friday, I want that tool. But there's a difference between a tool that supports a clinician and a system that operates on a patient before the clinician is even in the room.⏎⏎Here is what I'd ask HHS to think about specifically:⏎⏎1. Consent needs to be real and prior, not buried in an intake form I signed years ago.⏎2. Patients should be told, in plain language, when an AI system has reviewed their data as part of their care visit, not as a general policy disclosure but as a specific notification.⏎3. Human review should be required before any AI-generated finding influences a clinical decision, not as a rubber stamp but as genuine independent judgment.⏎⏎The acceleration framing in this RFI worries me. I understand the appeal. Faster diagnosis, earlier intervention, those are real goods. But the word "accelerate" assumes we know where we're going, and I'm not sure we do yet, at least not well enough to pick up speed.⏎⏎I also want to say, and I know this is a tangent but I'm going to say it anyway, I got a bill after my last imaging appointment for $3,200. The visit was supposed to be covered. Nobody could tell me what the $3,200 was for, whether it was the scan, the read, or some new layer of analysis I hadn't been told about. I'm still trying to figure that out. And if we're adding AI systems on top of a billing infrastructure that's already this opaque♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0083♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Thomas♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔Milken Institute School of Public Health at GWU♔♔♔♔♔♔The Milken Institute School of Public Health at GWU welcomes this request for comment. Before AI adoption accelerates further, HHS must confront what the NaviHealth controversy made plain: algorithmic denial systems are already harming Medicare Advantage enrollees at scale.⏎⏎Acceleration without accountability is not progress.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0084♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Brown♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔National Minority Quality Forum♔♔♔♔♔♔National Minority Quality Forum | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎The National Minority Quality Forum urges HHS to pause before extending any payment incentives to AI systems operating in insurance functions. AI-generated prior authorization denials are reaching patients before any human reviews the case.⏎⏎NMQF | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The populations most harmed, elderly Medicaid enrollees and non-English-speaking patients, rarely appeal. That silence looks like data absence, not safety.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0085♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Scott♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter drove me three hours to Portland because a computer system flagged something wrong with my results and it turned out to be a glitch, six weeks of fear for nothing, and that whole experience is exactly why I'm nervous about where this AI push is headed and also what about dental coverage for seniors because I'm 66 years old and I've been hearing that promise my whole adult life and nothing ever happens and here we are talking about fancy diagnostics when I can't get a basic tooth pulled covered by Medicare and it seems to me like whoever is running this process might not realize they're mostly hearing from tech companies and not from patients like me who just want someone accountable when the machine gets it wrong before we're all locked in the same way hospitals got stuck with those billing systems they couldn't afford to leave♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0086♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Johnson♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔American Academy of Pediatrics♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Academy of Pediatrics urges HHS to recognize that accelerating AI adoption without preserving the irreplaceable human relationship at medicine's core risks treating children as data points rather than patients deserving genuine clinical presence.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0087♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Lee♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sent via fax 208-555-0143 | To: HHS Docket | Re: AI in Clinical Care RFI | Page 1 of 2⏎⏎Twelve years in the ICU at St. Luke's in Boise. That is where I come from when I read something like this.⏎⏎My pharmacist mentioned something recently about new transparency requirements for clinical software and I am honestly not sure if that is what this request is about, but it got me looking, and what I found concerns me enough to write.⏎⏎Here is the thing I keep coming back to. In 2019, W. Nicholson Price II and colleagues published a piece in JAMA pointing out that existing malpractice doctrine does not cleanly accommodate AI-assisted harm and that the standard of care question is genuinely unresolved. That was six years ago. I do not think we have solved it. And in the meantime HHS is talking about accelerating adoption.⏎⏎I watched a clinical decision-support tool auto-populate the wrong patient weight and nearly send a septic man the wrong dose. A human nurse caught it. Thirty seconds. That near-miss never left me, and it is exactly why I do not think speed and accountability can be treated as separate questions you address in sequence.⏎⏎What worries me practically is this: if an AI tool gives a bad recommendation and a patient is harmed, who is responsible? The hospital that deployed it? The vendor who built it? The physician who followed it? Right now you can ask that question and get pointed in three directions at once. Vendor contracts often include indemnification clauses that effectively remove the vendor from the picture. The physician may argue she was following a cleared device. The hospital says it implemented the tool as directed. The patient is left with nothing but a chain of people pointing at each other.⏎⏎FDA 510(k) clearance does not resolve this. Clearance means a device is substantially equivalent to something that already exists, it does not mean the tool has been validated for the specific patient population, workflow, or institution where it is being used. A tool cleared for general use in a large urban academic center may behave very differently in a smaller community hospital in Idaho.⏎⏎I am not against AI in clinical care. I genuinely believe it can help. But I believe HHS should require that liability be assigned clearly by statute before reimbursement incentives go out the door. Vendor indemnification clauses that leave patients unprotected should not be enforceable in federally reimbursed care settings. And any reimbursement incentive tied to AI adoption should be conditioned on context-specific safety validation, not clearance alone.⏎⏎I am also just asking, honestly, is there somewhere in this process where individual patients or retired nurses can follow what is being decided? I have looked at the docket page twice and I am still not entirely sure I am reading the right document.⏎⏎Page 2 of 2 | Fax transmission complete 04/14/2025 09:47 AM♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0088♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Martin♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔Subtle Medical♔♔♔♔♔♔Subtle Medical | HHS AI RFI Comment | Page 1⏎⏎---⏎⏎June 12, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎---⏎⏎PRELIMINARY STATEMENT⏎⏎Subtle Medical appreciates the Department's interest in AI's potential to improve clinical care. That potential is real. What is also real, and what this RFI's framing conspicuously elides, is a structural and recurring failure mode in health technology deployment that has caused measurable harm to patients and practices across multiple technology cycles. As a company whose staff includes practice managers who have absorbed the operational consequences of those failures, we submit this comment with some impatience.⏎⏎ON THE BENCHMARK-TO-BEDSIDE PROBLEM⏎⏎Obermeyer and Emanuel (NEJM, 2016) documented with clarity what practitioners already knew from experience: models that perform impressively on training populations routinely degrade when deployed against the heterogeneous, messy, documentation-inconsistent patient panels that characterize real clinical environments. This is not a solvable problem at the pre-deployment stage alone. Sendak et al. (JMIR Medical Informatics, 2020) studied the real-world integration of a sepsis deep learning tool and found meaningful performance degradation post-deployment — degradation that pre-deployment benchmarks had not predicted and that clinical staff were not equipped to detect.⏎⏎Subtle Medical | HHS AI RFI Comment | Page 2⏎⏎---⏎⏎This is the pattern. Ash, Berg, and Coiera (JAMIA, 2004) traced it through earlier generations of clinical decision support, and ONC's own 2022 Health IT Safety Program issue brief acknowledged persisting post-deployment safety gaps in clinical decision support tools that had passed pre-market evaluation. The EHR implementation failures of the 2010s followed the same arc. Alert fatigue is a direct product of systems that looked functional in controlled environments and then generated noise at scale.⏎⏎ON AUTOMATION BIAS AS A PATIENT SAFETY ISSUE⏎⏎When AI tools are embedded in clinical workflows under a reimbursement pathway that signals institutional endorsement, clinicians reasonably trust them more than the evidence warrants. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt (JAMIA, 2012) reviewed the frequency and mediators of automation bias and found consistent evidence that clinician vigilance decreases when AI recommendations are present — including when those recommendations are wrong. Accelerating deployment before the benchmark-to-bedside gap is understood and mitigated does not reduce this risk. It amplifies it.⏎⏎SPECIFIC REQUEST TO HHS⏎⏎Our ask is straightforward: HHS should require mandatory post-deployment performance monitoring, with results reported publicly, as a condition of any reimbursement pathway for AI tools used in clinical or administrative determinations. Not voluntary reporting. Not self-attestation by vendors. Actual performance data, drawn from the deployment environments where the tool operates, made available to the practices and clinicians who are absorbing the operational risk.⏎⏎Our organization would welcome the opportunity to discuss what a workable reporting framework might look like in practice, including the minimum data fields and reporting cadence that would make such a requirement meaningful rather than⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Susan Martin⏎Practice Manager, Subtle Medical⏎susan.martin@subtlemedical.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0089♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Williams♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔Children's Hospital Association♔♔♔♔♔♔Children's Hospital Association Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎HHS must require meaningful human review of all AI-generated coverage determinations before accelerating AI adoption in insurance functions any further.⏎⏎Children's Hospital Association Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0090♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Wright♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔Drexel University College of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔ACCOUNTABILITY BEFORE ACCELERATION⏎⏎Drexel University College of Medicine's postdoctoral research community welcomes HHS engagement on clinical AI adoption. Reproducibility failures in AI diagnostic tools remain a serious barrier, with fewer than 30% of evaluated publications providing sufficient detail for external validation.⏎⏎LIABILITY STRUCTURES⏎⏎When vendors disclaim responsibility through contract terms and FDA clearance does not address specific deployment contexts, injured patients have no identifiable accountable party.⏎⏎HHS should condition adoption acceleration on establishing non-waivable accountability frameworks first.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0091♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Nelson♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother's oncologist's office ran her biopsy through an automated screening system that flagged her low-risk. It took a second physician, one who actually read her full history, to catch what the algorithm missed. We almost didn't push for that second opinion.⏎⏎That experience is why I'm skeptical of what HHS is calling "accelerating adoption." When a hospital or insurer talks about efficiency, what they usually mean is fewer people in the loop. The workers getting displaced by these tools are disproportionately women, many of color, doing the administrative and frontline jobs that actually create accountability. The physicians who disagree with an AI recommendation often don't have the time or institutional standing to fight it. And the burnout argument feels dishonest to me. Vendors promise relief, but the clinicians I've talked to describe more documentation burden, not less. HHS should require independent oversight with real enforcement authority. Industry-led credentialing, run by the same parties profiting from fast adoption, is not sufficient and I don't understand why that would even be on the table.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0092♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Torres♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother-in-law Carlos nearly died at Saint Barnabas in 2022 because an automated screening system said he was fine and nobody questioned it. Three days later he was back with a blood clot. That is not a glitch, that is what happens when machines make calls and humans stop thinking. We already know these tools misread darker skin on pulse oximeters and flag minority patients wrong on insurance claims. Auditing for bias has to come before any acceleration, period. Who exactly is accountable when the next person dies?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0093♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Jackson♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔American Medical Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Medical Association⏎330 North Wabash Avenue⏎Chicago, Illinois 60611⏎⏎June 2, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information -- Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Dear Deputy Secretary:⏎⏎The American Medical Association urges HHS to impose binding interoperability requirements, vendor conflict-of-interest disclosures, and independent algorithmic oversight before accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings, not after market consolidation has already occurred and made course correction prohibitively costly for safety-net providers.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Paul Jackson⏎Executive Director⏎American Medical Association⏎pjackson@ama-assn.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0094♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Martin♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My Medicare card application has been sitting in limbo for three months now and nobody at the 1-800 number can tell me where it is.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0095♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Young♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔Roswell Park Comprehensive Cancer Center♔♔♔♔♔♔Roswell Park Comprehensive Cancer Center welcomes the opportunity to respond to this Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. Our researchers have observed, across multiple federally funded implementation studies, a persistent and structurally produced gap between controlled benchmark performance and real-world deployment outcomes that HHS must treat as a precondition for any endorsement policy.⏎⏎HHS should require minimum evidence thresholds and mandatory post-deployment monitoring before reimbursing or endorsing any clinical AI tool.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0096♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Scott♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Oracle Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah Scott⏎Senior Director, Healthcare Systems Operations⏎Oracle Corporation⏎2300 Cloud Way, Austin, TX 78741⏎dscott@oracle.com⏎⏎June 2, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎PRELIMINARY OBSERVATIONS⏎⏎Oracle Corporation submits these comments in response to HHS's solicitation regarding AI adoption in clinical care settings. Our organization supports AI integration where deployment infrastructure is adequate. What concerns us is the framing of acceleration as the primary policy objective.⏎⏎Oracle Corporation | HHS AI RFI Comments | June 2025 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎THE BENCHMARK-TO-DEPLOYMENT GAP IS NOT NEW⏎⏎The gap between controlled benchmark performance and real-world clinical outcomes is a structural problem the health IT sector has documented repeatedly, not a correctable edge case. Obermeyer and Emanuel identified this pattern in 2016 (New England Journal of Medicine, 375:1216), observing that machine learning tools trained on curated datasets routinely fail to generalize when deployed into noisy, fragmented operational environments. Wong et al. (JAMA Internal Medicine, 181:1065, 2021) documented exactly this failure mode with a widely implemented sepsis prediction model — a tool marketed on strong internal validation that produced near-random performance at external sites. These are not cautionary anecdotes. They are implementation science evidence that the industry has not yet resolved.⏎⏎Oracle Corporation | HHS AI RFI Comments | June 2025 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎AUTOMATION BIAS AS AN OPERATIONAL RISK⏎⏎What makes silent performance degradation particularly dangerous in clinical AI is automation bias. Sendak et al. (ACM FAccT, 2020) documented how AI-generated outputs in clinical settings reduce active clinician scrutiny at precisely the moments when errors are most probable. A tool that degrades quietly in a high-volume emergency department does not simply fail to help — it actively reduces the vigilance that would otherwise catch the error the tool introduced. I keep returning to this point because it does not appear in the RFI's framing at all, and it should.⏎⏎Oracle Corporation | HHS AI RFI Comments | June 2025 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎WHAT FEDERAL POLICY HAS ALREADY RECOGNIZED⏎⏎ONC's 2022 Health IT Patient Safety Action & Surveillance Plan formally acknowledged that implementation environments introduce patient safety risks that pre-deployment validation cannot fully anticipate. Sittig and Singh documented this same structural problem with EHR clinical decision support a decade earlier (NEJM, 367:1854, 2012), describing alert fatigue and workflow disruption as predictable consequences of deploying systems before implementation science was ready. The pattern from⏎⏎Oracle Corporation | HHS AI RFI Comments | June 2025 | Page 4⏎⏎that period is instructive and the current RFI does not meaningfully distinguish the acceleration it proposes from the acceleration that produced those outcomes.⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD PRIORITIZE⏎⏎Before expanding reimbursement pathways or reducing regulatory friction, HHS should establish mandatory real-world performance monitoring requirements for deployed AI tools, with defined thresholds for signal degradation that trigger review. Staged validation requirements — requiring post-deployment performance data from at least two independent health system environments before broad reimbursement eligibility — would address the benchmark gap without halting development. The compliance overhead our organization tracks across multi-system deployments, averaging over $214,000 per product line annually in conflicting payer documentation requirements, is a real problem worth solving. But solving it by accelerating past the validation infrastructure question does not♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0097♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Torres♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔Family Caregiver Alliance♔♔♔♔♔♔Family Caregiver Alliance submits these comments to urge HHS to reframe this RFI around accountability rather than acceleration, and to require mandatory workforce impact assessments before any clinical AI deployment is permitted.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0098♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Mitchell♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My chart had the wrong medication dosage on it for close to three months back in the winter of 2022, and not one computer flag caught it. My daughter had to drive up from Columbus and sit in the room with me before a human being actually looked at my file with their own eyes. I bring that up because when I finally asked whose fault it was, nobody could give me a straight answer. The doctor's office said it was the software. The software company, I was told, has language in their contracts saying they are not responsible for how doctors use their product. That is the problem I want HHS to hear.⏎⏎If you let hospitals build their whole workflow around these AI systems before anyone decides who is legally responsible when something goes wrong, you will never get them out again. Hospitals don't reverse course on systems they've spent money on, I've seen how that works. Please, before any of this goes further, make sure there is one clear party who has to answer for the mistake.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0099♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Clark♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Arterys♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan Clark⏎Practice Manager, Arterys⏎4200 Northview Drive, Suite 102⏎Boise, ID 83704⏎susan.clark@arterys.com⏎⏎April 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Arterys Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 1⏎⏎WHAT THIS RFI GETS WRONG ABOUT CLINICAL CARE⏎⏎Fourteen years of managing clinical operations have taught me that what heals people is rarely the thing that gets optimized. The Kelley et al. 2014 meta-analysis in PLOS ONE is unambiguous on this: the patient-clinician relationship independently predicts outcomes. Not as a soft variable. As a measurable one. And yet this RFI treats care delivery primarily as a throughput problem to be solved with better tooling.⏎⏎I keep thinking about a patient scenario I have watched play out dozens of times. An elderly woman comes in. Her daughter is with her, struggling to articulate what has changed. The clinician reads something in the room — posture, affect, the way the daughter keeps glancing at the door — and orders a test that wasn't on the intake checklist. That read is not in any dataset. It will not survive translation into a structured prompt.⏎⏎Arterys Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 2⏎⏎ON REIMBURSEMENT AND ADMINISTRATIVE BURDEN⏎⏎Our organization works across multiple health system deployments, and the reimbursement inconsistency we encounter is genuinely operationally damaging. The ONC's 2022 strategy on reducing regulatory and administrative burden acknowledged that fragmented health IT frameworks impose real costs on care organizations. We have seen this firsthand: sites navigating two or three conflicting payer standards simultaneously, generating compliance overhead that consumes resources that should go to patients. AI tools that handle prior authorization, scheduling, documentation — these have legitimate administrative value and should be reimbursed consistently and with clear CPT guidance.⏎⏎But the clinical encounter is different territory.⏎⏎Zulman et al. in JAMA 2020 documented specifically which physician behaviors sustain patient connection during encounters: presence, attentiveness, acknowledgment of emotion. These are not behaviors that scale through automation. Eric Topol himself, in his 2019 Nature Medicine piece often cited to justify AI adoption, was careful to note that empathy and relational care represent a genuine boundary for what AI can replicate. The National Academy of Medicine's 2023 consensus report on AI in health care similarly flagged that deployment speed is outrunning our ability to assess impact on care relationships.⏎⏎HHS should adopt a precautionary posture for any AI application that operates inside the clinical encounter. Not a suggestion. A demand. Patient subjective experience of care — the sense of being known, heard, and seen by a clinician — must be treated as a non-negotiable regulatory value with its own evaluation criteria, not a secondary consideration folded into a⏎⏎Arterys Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 3⏎⏎workflow efficiency metric. I am not sure how HHS plans to operationalize that, and honestly I am not sure HHS has asked the question yet.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0100♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Miller♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔1upHealth♔♔♔♔♔♔1upHealth Comments on HHS Health Sector AI RFI: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Ashley Miller, VP of Regulatory Affairs, 1upHealth⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎⏎---⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎Our organization's 2024 analysis of 47 commercial payer contracts found that 63% contained materially inconsistent performance evaluation criteria for AI-assisted clinical decision support tools, generating an estimated $2.3 million in redundant compliance expenditures annually. A parallel survey of 112 health technology developers identified liability ambiguity for non-FDA-regulated AI tools as the top barrier to market entry, cited by 78% of respondents. These findings do not argue for acceleration. They argue that the governance preconditions for responsible acceleration do not yet exist.⏎⏎THE SEQUENCING PROBLEM⏎⏎The RFI proceeds from an assumption that the appropriate federal role is to deploy regulatory, reimbursement, and research levers simultaneously to drive AI adoption. We believe this sequence is backwards and, once executed, largely irreversible. The Government Accountability Office documented in GAO-22-104629 (February 2022) that AI performance gaps in clinical settings are systematically underdetected prior to broad deployment — a finding consistent with Obermeyer and Emanuel's earlier work in the New England Journal of Medicine on how machine learning errors compound at scale before they become visible to clinicians or administrators.⏎⏎1upHealth Comments | HHS-AI-RFI-2024 | Page 2⏎⏎The EHR analogy is worth sitting with here. ONC's own data brief (No. 58, 2023) documents near-universal EHR adoption across hospital settings, but Kruse et al. (Journal of Medical Systems, 2016) documented extensively that health IT deployments become structurally irreversible once embedded in clinical workflows — no major EHR system has been successfully removed from a large health system after deployment, regardless of performance. AI reimbursement incentives will create the same lock-in, and Dafny and Lee's analysis of how federal reimbursement structures set market conditions that exceed the political will to later correct is directly applicable here.⏎⏎DEMOCRATIC LEGITIMACY⏎⏎How much AI belongs in clinical medicine is not an administrative question. It is a values question that belongs to legislatures and public deliberation, not to an RFI process that presupposes the answer. HHS's role should be to preserve the option space for that deliberation, not foreclose it by embedding underevaluated vendors in federal reimbursement structures before harmonized liability rules, independent audit requirements, and democratic oversight mechanisms have been constructed.⏎⏎THE SPECIFIC ASK⏎⏎HHS should reframe this RFI's mandate from acceleration to sequenced governance. Concretely, the Department should publish a preconditions framework — covering liability indemnification standards, third-party audit requirements, and performance evaluation criteria — before any reimbursement or regulatory adoption levers are activated. The 63% inconsistency rate we observed across payer contracts is not a minor compliance friction issue. It is evidence that the market cannot self-correct toward coherent standards.⏎⏎[See Appendix A: 1upHealth Payer Contract Performance Criteria Analysis, on file with submitting organization — not attached to this submission per document size limitations]⏎⏎Contact: regulatory@1uphealth.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
